WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:06.509
The second season of You is packed with easter
eggs including secret Beck and Benji references,

00:00:06.509 --> 00:00:11.990
Forty discovering the truth about Peach Salinger's
murder, plus the iconic carpet from The Shining.

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:12.990
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers!

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:17.440
I'm Jan and in this video I'm revealing 18
killer details you might have missed in Season

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:18.440
2 of You.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:21.780
There will, of course, be spoilers so take
care if you haven't caught up.

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:26.680
LA life and its obsession with health and
wellness in particular are satirised in Season

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:32.460
2, especially by Joe, who coming from New
York finds Angelenos particularly irritating.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:36.500
Interestingly though, Moon Juice which Forty
declared essential to safely tripping on LSD,

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:41.710
was actually referenced in an amusing moment
in Season 1 when Joe's colleague Ethan raves

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:43.239
about the Moon Juice cookbook.

00:00:43.239 --> 00:00:47.220
"Amanda Chantal Bacon has a dope recipe in
'Moon Juice Cookbook: Cook Cosmically for

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:48.300
Body, Beauty, and Consciousness.'"

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:49.300
[Joe] Kill me.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:50.300
Sounds great, Ethan."

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:54.510
Another Season 1 call-back pops up after Joe
and Love break up and Ellie sends him a Top

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:55.920
10 list of Dating Apps.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:01.120
At number two is "LoveHooks", an app co-created
by one of Joe's previous victims.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:02.690
"Benjamin J. Ashby III.

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:04.110
Two failed careers.

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:05.560
Model... oh, boy.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:10.170
And cocreator of a dating app that connects
people through musical tastes called LoveHooks."

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:14.320
One of the best hidden links to Joe's past
life in New York is the name of the store

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:16.850
where he meets Love for the first time: Anavrin.

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:21.119
"Obviously, Anavrin is wait for it "nirvana"
spelled backwards."

00:01:21.119 --> 00:01:25.119
"Nirvana" is actually the name of the store
right next to Mooney's bookstore back in New

00:01:25.119 --> 00:01:26.130
York.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:29.119
Beck also wore a Nirvana T-shirt in Season
1.

00:01:29.119 --> 00:01:33.909
And, in one of the second season flashbacks,
Joe's mother is also wearing that same T-shirt

00:01:33.909 --> 00:01:34.909
design.

00:01:34.909 --> 00:01:39.939
So, it seems all this is likely a hint that
Joe is especially drawn to things he connects,

00:01:39.939 --> 00:01:42.329
maybe unconsciously, to his mother.

00:01:42.329 --> 00:01:46.270
The original meaning of the word "nirvana"
is a state of bliss and tranquillity.

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:50.219
And the fact that Anavrin is the opposite
of this word might be the first indication

00:01:50.219 --> 00:01:54.229
that the Quinns who own the grocery store
are not quite as lovely as they might first

00:01:54.229 --> 00:01:55.229
seem.

00:01:55.229 --> 00:01:59.479
The drug-fuelled eighth episode of Season
2 was particularly crazy, and there were also

00:01:59.479 --> 00:02:03.060
numerous fascinating easter eggs which the
showrunners and production designers snuck

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:04.060
into the episode.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:08.599
A version of The Shining hotel's carpet with
its characteristic hexagonal pattern appears

00:02:08.599 --> 00:02:13.000
in the corridors of the hotel where Forty
takes Joe and Ellie to work on his screenplay,

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:17.880
which seems especially fitting as Jack Nicholson's
character also uses The Shining's hotel as

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:19.010
a place to write.

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:22.920
The scene where you can spot the carpet plays
out in a similar way to the iconic vision

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.590
of the twins that Danny has in The Shining.

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:30.930
In You, Joe is out of his mind on Forty's
spiked LSD, and as he turns the corner of

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:35.450
the hotel's corridor he sees a vision of Candace,
which actually turns out to be Dimitri.

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:39.880
As you can see, the colour of the carpet is
different compared to the one in The Shining,

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:42.420
but it's the same distinctive pattern.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:47.150
The second Shining reference comes from the
hotel room that Forty locks Joe into as the

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:52.140
room number 217 is the same number as the
haunted room in Stephen King's original

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:53.180
novel.

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:57.530
In that haunted room in The Shining, Jack
Torrance sees a vision of a creepy woman in

00:02:57.530 --> 00:02:58.760
a bathtub.

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:04.180
And during his LSD trip, Joe also sees a ghostly
vision of his mother appear to him while he's

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:06.510
freaking out in the bathtub.

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:11.190
And similar to how the ghosts in the Overlook
Hotel incite Jack's violent rage, so too does

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:15.560
the vision of Joe's mother spur him on to
almost murder Forty.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:20.870
It was Forty's sudden realisation that Beck
was killed by her ex-boyfriend, not Dr. Nicky,

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:23.240
that led Joe to almost kill Love's brother.

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:28.569
But even before this ultimate revelation,
Forty was also figuring out other secret details

00:03:28.569 --> 00:03:32.130
about Beck which we can spot by looking closely
at his whiteboard.

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:36.670
Here, we can see that Forty has also figured
out that Peach didn't commit suicide, as the

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:41.190
official version of events state, but that
she was murdered.

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:45.600
Joe is well aware in Season 2 that Peach's
murder could come back to bite him one day

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:50.190
given the Salinger family had a P.I. investigating
her death at the end of Season 1 and that

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:54.750
he accidentally left behind evidence that
he was at Peach's family home in Greenwich.

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:56.330
"What if they take DNA?

00:03:56.330 --> 00:04:00.270
Not like there's a crusty jar of piss in Peach
Salinger's house or anything."

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:04.490
Joe's paranoia about the Salingers tracking
him down may well have revealed itself when

00:04:04.490 --> 00:04:09.180
he went back to Anavrin for snacks and Moon
Juice during his crazy LSD-filled adventures.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:12.959
The next day, when Joe asks Calvin to tell
him what happened when he found him in the

00:04:12.959 --> 00:04:17.489
store in the middle of the night, Calvin says:
"Okay, you gotta remember the part where you

00:04:17.489 --> 00:04:20.870
were ranting about how all the peaches were
mad at you."

00:04:20.870 --> 00:04:24.810
And it's no coincidence either that during
his first encounter with Love, she jokes to

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:27.689
him:
"Does this peach look like a butt?"

00:04:27.689 --> 00:04:32.130
There's a funny bit of meta-commentary about
You's first season when Ellie passes critical

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:36.960
judgement over Forty's script for his adaption
of Beck's book 'The Dark Face of Love'.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.009
"First off, the script is predictable."

00:04:39.009 --> 00:04:41.099
"I was staying true to the source material."

00:04:41.099 --> 00:04:43.180
"And the female perspective is sorely lacking."

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:44.240
"Beck was real.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:46.360
She humps a pillow multiple times!"

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:50.389
It's a humorous recognition by the show's
own writers that, like the original book,

00:04:50.389 --> 00:04:55.069
almost everything in You is told from Joe's
point-of-view, with one small exception in

00:04:55.069 --> 00:04:59.669
the fourth episode of Season 1 where some
of the episode was told from Beck's perspective.

00:04:59.669 --> 00:05:01.490
"What was that?

00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:02.740
Eight seconds?"

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:07.949
There's also a joke about the somewhat ridiculous
alias Candace uses to hide her identity when

00:05:07.949 --> 00:05:11.279
she arrives in LA:
"You also said your name was 'Amy Adam', which,

00:05:11.279 --> 00:05:14.139
come to think of it, sounds really stupid.

00:05:14.139 --> 00:05:17.400
What, was 'Britney Spear' already taken?"

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:21.370
The name "Amy Adam" is also an easter egg
to the book "Hidden Bodies" which Season 2

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:22.490
is based on.

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:26.199
In the novel, Amy is a completely separate
character from Candace.

00:05:26.199 --> 00:05:30.559
Basically, she's an ex-girlfriend of Joe's
who runs away from New York after she scams

00:05:30.559 --> 00:05:31.559
him.

00:05:31.559 --> 00:05:35.330
Joe then leaves New York to hunt Amy down
with the intention of killing her.

00:05:35.330 --> 00:05:41.650
The TV show neatly reverses this by having
Amy, aka Candace, track Joe down instead.

00:05:41.650 --> 00:05:45.639
The show also gets to play around with our
expectations of what will happen by the end

00:05:45.639 --> 00:05:50.121
of Season 2 by getting Forty to deliver a
little red herring early on in the season

00:05:50.121 --> 00:05:53.849
when he sits down with Joe to discuss his
script, "Bang Marry Kill".

00:05:53.849 --> 00:05:58.849
"In the end, our guy has to murder all three
women.

00:05:58.849 --> 00:06:01.310
It's like...
the ultimate irony."

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:05.370
Now, when you watch this for the first time,
you might think it means that Joe will end

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:10.460
up killing the three main women in his life
right now: Candace, Delilah, and Love.

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:14.699
Especially when you think back to how he basically
did the same to three women in Season 1: Beck,

00:06:14.699 --> 00:06:17.279
Peach, and Candace, who Joe assumed he'd killed.

00:06:17.279 --> 00:06:22.389
However, it's a bit of a red herring, as the
surprise villain of the second season is Love

00:06:22.389 --> 00:06:25.229
who ends up killing Candace and Delilah.

00:06:25.229 --> 00:06:29.729
And if we add that she also killed her family's
au pair Sophia, that makes Love the one to

00:06:29.729 --> 00:06:32.560
kill three women by the end.

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:36.749
Season 2's writers also have some fun with
the famous dramatic principle of "Chekhov's

00:06:36.749 --> 00:06:40.590
Gun", which, for anyone who's unfamiliar,
is the basic idea that every element in a

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:42.589
story should serve a purpose.

00:06:42.589 --> 00:06:46.870
And that, for example, if you draw attention
to a gun, then that gun must go off at some

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:48.479
point in the story.

00:06:48.479 --> 00:06:53.039
In You it's used as a big clue to who the
secret killer of Season 2 will actually be

00:06:53.039 --> 00:06:57.490
when Forty begins to drunkenly explain his
feature film idea at Henderson's party.

00:06:57.490 --> 00:06:58.490
"It's Chekhov's knife.

00:06:58.490 --> 00:07:02.789
And as soon as we see it, then you know throats
are gonna get cut.

00:07:02.789 --> 00:07:07.039
But we don't know whose, and we don't know
why.

00:07:07.039 --> 00:07:09.070
She's cutting an apple right now.

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:13.960
But the audience is gonna be subconsciously
terrified..."

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:17.360
This is a major hint that when we see Love
expertly use a knife to butcher a piece of

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:20.340
meat, we should realise that she will end
up butchering several people by the end of

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:21.340
the season.

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:22.520
"Now hold your knife like this."

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:23.539
"Up here?"

00:07:23.539 --> 00:07:24.539
"Mm-hmm."

00:07:24.539 --> 00:07:28.180
By the way, we owe the principle of "Chekhov's
Gun" to influential Russian playwright and

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:33.759
short story writer Anton Chekhov who hated
any element in a story making "false promises"

00:07:33.759 --> 00:07:34.919
to the audience.

00:07:34.919 --> 00:07:39.120
And the writers of You joke around with this
idea when Forty tells Joe about how his script's

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:43.069
developing:
"I don't think False Promises is gonna happen.

00:07:43.069 --> 00:07:45.440
It's a short story, not a feature film."

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:50.119
"Chekhov's Gun" also pops up when Joe breaks
in to Henderson's basement looking for evidence

00:07:50.119 --> 00:07:52.080
of his crimes against underage girls.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:55.800
There he discovers a robot vacuum cleaner
that starts up automatically.

00:07:55.800 --> 00:08:00.710
In a case of "Chekhov's Roomba", the device
comes into play on Joe's next visit after

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:05.669
he kills Henderson, when it switches on automatically
and mixes Henderson's blood with Joe's blood

00:08:05.669 --> 00:08:07.539
from his nosebleed.

00:08:07.539 --> 00:08:11.439
The psychological thriller Gone Girl gets
some interesting references in this season

00:08:11.439 --> 00:08:12.439
of You.

00:08:12.439 --> 00:08:16.529
First of all, there's a very deliberate decision
to name Delilah's police officer hook-up..

00:08:16.529 --> 00:08:17.529
"David..

00:08:17.529 --> 00:08:18.529
Fincher.."

00:08:18.529 --> 00:08:22.039
That's the same name as the director of Gone
Girl, a little detail that doesn't escape

00:08:22.039 --> 00:08:23.350
Joe's observation.

00:08:23.350 --> 00:08:26.149
"Like Officer other David Fincher."

00:08:26.149 --> 00:08:30.069
And the ending of the second season also has
numerous parallels with the ending of Gone

00:08:30.069 --> 00:08:31.169
Girl.

00:08:31.169 --> 00:08:34.440
Spoilers for that movie ahead, but you can
skip to this timestamp in case you haven't

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:35.660
seen it.

00:08:35.660 --> 00:08:40.060
In the final episode, Love tells Joe she's
pregnant, which is the only thing that stops

00:08:40.060 --> 00:08:44.410
him from killing her and leaving, similar
to how Rosamund Pike reveals she's pregnant

00:08:44.410 --> 00:08:48.480
to Ben Affleck with his child at the end of
Gone Girl, which again is the only reason

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:50.590
he decides to stay with her.

00:08:50.590 --> 00:08:55.400
And the twist in both Gone Girl and You Season
2 is that the main female protagonist is revealed

00:08:55.400 --> 00:09:01.250
to be a psychopath or sociopath who secretly
engineered numerous key events in the story.

00:09:01.250 --> 00:09:05.600
Office Fincher is also used for a humorous
reference to a famous TV detective when Joe

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:09.100
snarkily comments that Fincher "operates with
all the nuance of Columbo."

00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:13.910
And true to Joe's observation, in the final
episode, before Fincher lets Ellie leave police

00:09:13.910 --> 00:09:16.990
custody, he uses Columbo's trademark catchphrase.

00:09:16.990 --> 00:09:18.640
"Oh, and one more thing."

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:20.310
"Oh, one more thing Sir.

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:21.310
Oh!

00:09:21.310 --> 00:09:22.310
One more thing.

00:09:22.310 --> 00:09:23.420
Just one more thing Sir.

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:24.800
Yes Lieutenant?"

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:28.350
The final scene of Season 2 is clearly setting
up the third season.

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:32.670
But there's a very subtle musical and audio
easter egg in this scene that signals Joe's

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:35.940
about to have his attention drawn to yet another
woman.

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:39.990
You need to listen very carefully because
as Joe walks out into the garden and thinks

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:43.990
about his fate, there's a little bell sound
in the music that reminds me of the doorbell

00:09:43.990 --> 00:09:48.040
at Mooney's that rang when Beck first entered
his life in the pilot episode.

00:09:48.040 --> 00:09:51.740
"But that's not destiny works, is it?"

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:53.120
[Bells ring.]

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:55.210
"Well, hello there."

00:09:55.210 --> 00:09:59.760
That doorbell also rang when Candace came
back into Joe's life at the end of Season

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:04.300
1 and a little bell is now ringing for Joe
at the end of Season 2 as a brand new woman

00:10:04.300 --> 00:10:07.550
seems set to become the object of his obsession.

00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:11.571
One of the earliest clues the writers laid
for us about the ending of Season 2 was right

00:10:11.571 --> 00:10:15.500
in the opening scene of the first episode
when we see the body of a red-headed actress

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:19.960
on a film set lying in a pool of blood, complete
with Candace's signature leather jacket and

00:10:19.960 --> 00:10:21.150
heart-shaped sunglasses.

00:10:21.150 --> 00:10:26.740
And I go into that and all of the other secret
clues about Love's dark nature in my Ending

00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:28.810
and Twist Explained video.

00:10:28.810 --> 00:10:32.160
Tap the screen here or there's a link you
can follow in the video description.

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:36.710
So, did you spot any other cool details or
easter eggs in Season 2?

00:10:36.710 --> 00:10:39.420
And what were your favourite moments or new
characters?

